# PART 1 — PROJECT ARCHITECTURE AND CORE PRINCIPLES

## 1.1 Two separate experiences

Build two connected but securely separated interfaces.

**A. Public website**

- Arabic language.
- Full right-to-left layout.
- Accessible to anyone worldwide through the public URL.
- No visitor registration.
- No visitor login.
- No Google account requirement.
- No project collaborator requirement.
- No invitation requirement.
- Visitors can browse all publicly published content.
- Public visitors must never be able to access the owner dashboard.

**B. Owner administration dashboard**

- Accessible only through `/admin`.
- Arabic RTL interface.
- Fully protected authentication.
- Complete owner permissions.
- Comprehensive administration menu.
- Every website feature manageable through the dashboard.
- Changes saved persistently and applied to the public website.

The public website and the administration dashboard must use the same underlying data and settings system, but access permissions must be separated securely.

## 1.2 Essential development principles

1. Build real functionality, not static mockups.
2. Use a persistent database for pages, content, settings, metadata, and user activity.
3. Use persistent cloud/object storage for uploaded files.
4. Do not rely on browser local storage as the only permanent storage.
5. Use secure server-side authorization.
6. Keep the original uploaded media safe.
7. Avoid duplicate records and duplicate uploads.
8. Do not lose data when refreshing, logging out, logging in, or redeploying.
9. Use reusable components and centralized configuration.
10. Keep page-specific settings separate from global settings.
11. Make the system responsive on desktop, tablet, and mobile.
12. Provide meaningful error handling and recovery.
13. Create backups before major changes.
14. Maintain a version history for settings and important modifications.
15. Do not claim a feature works until it has been tested.

